1
00:00:00,088 --> 00:00:04,295
Imagine deleting a free game because
your console needs the space.

2
00:00:04,575 --> 00:00:06,608
Usually, that's a boring decision.

3
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:10,582
You open your library later,
download it again, and carry on.

4
00:00:10,875 --> 00:00:13,832
With P.T., that ordinary
expectation broke.

5
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:18,730
This is a real PlayStation Four download
error from GameSpot's test in May

6
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:19,658
twenty fifteen.

7
00:00:19,938 --> 00:00:22,145
The game wasn't just
missing from the shop.

8
00:00:22,438 --> 00:00:26,330
Even somebody who had downloaded it
before could find that the route back no

9
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:27,170
longer worked.

10
00:00:27,438 --> 00:00:31,182
Meanwhile, another console could
still have a playable installation.

11
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:35,107
Same game, different outcome, depending
on what was already on the drive.

12
00:00:35,412 --> 00:00:40,392
That's the strange afterlife of P.T.: a
horror teaser that made people afraid

13
00:00:40,412 --> 00:00:41,670
of a storage decision.

14
00:00:41,962 --> 00:00:46,768
To understand why anybody cared this much
about a free download, we need to go

15
00:00:46,788 --> 00:00:48,357
back to the room it put you in.

16
00:00:48,477 --> 00:00:49,497
You wake up on the floor.

17
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:54,657
There is a door ahead of you, and
beyond it, a corridor that looks almost

18
00:00:54,677 --> 00:00:56,610
disappointingly ordinary.

19
00:00:56,877 --> 00:01:00,935
A light fitting, family
photographs, a cabinet, a radio.

20
00:01:01,215 --> 00:01:04,795
The game gives you very little distance
to travel, so you start learning the

21
00:01:04,815 --> 00:01:06,347
details without trying.

22
00:01:06,627 --> 00:01:10,231
Then you reach the end, go through
the door, and arrive in that corridor

23
00:01:10,251 --> 00:01:10,683
again.

24
00:01:10,963 --> 00:01:13,971
The important part is what
changes on the next pass.

25
00:01:14,263 --> 00:01:16,321
A familiar picture has become a clue.

26
00:01:16,613 --> 00:01:18,408
You start wondering what else you missed.

27
00:01:18,713 --> 00:01:23,006
The light changes, and the same corner
suddenly feels like somewhere you

28
00:01:23,026 --> 00:01:24,046
shouldn't approach.

29
00:01:24,326 --> 00:01:28,756
Because the geography is familiar,
a small difference gets your full

30
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:29,396
attention.

31
00:01:29,688 --> 00:01:33,371
You can't dismiss it as another
room you haven't explored yet.

32
00:01:33,651 --> 00:01:34,218
P.T.

33
00:01:34,238 --> 00:01:37,671
keeps asking you to look more closely
at the place you already know.

34
00:01:37,976 --> 00:01:42,368
That creates a nasty little negotiation:
you want to make progress, but

35
00:01:42,388 --> 00:01:46,608
progress requires approaching the thing
that has just made you uncomfortable.

36
00:01:46,888 --> 00:01:50,896
Watch the camera pause at the closed
door, then check the room behind it.

37
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:53,270
The player has to decide
where to look next.

38
00:01:53,537 --> 00:01:57,555
A film can choose where your attention
goes; this makes that nervous

39
00:01:57,575 --> 00:01:59,257
inspection your responsibility.

40
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:03,324
The official PlayStation guide describes
that same bargain: examine the

41
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:07,451
surroundings, listen carefully, and
work out what lets you continue.

42
00:02:07,731 --> 00:02:12,552
It also acknowledges something less
elegant: people were solving parts of P.T.

43
00:02:12,645 --> 00:02:15,452
through trial and error,
or by working together.

44
00:02:15,745 --> 00:02:18,302
That uncertainty spread outside the game.

45
00:02:18,582 --> 00:02:23,025
Players compared clues and tried to
understand which actions actually

46
00:02:23,045 --> 00:02:23,602
mattered.

47
00:02:23,882 --> 00:02:27,700
Even a contemporary Game Informer
discussion asked readers whether they had

48
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:29,327
managed to finish the teaser.

49
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:32,415
The mystery wasn't only
inside the corridor.

50
00:02:32,695 --> 00:02:36,327
Nobody had initially been told
what this thing was advertising.

51
00:02:36,607 --> 00:02:41,262
At Gamescom in August twenty fourteen,
it appeared under the name seven

52
00:02:41,282 --> 00:02:43,327
seven eight zero s Studio.

53
00:02:43,595 --> 00:02:47,587
The announcement gave people a horror
experiment they could download

54
00:02:47,607 --> 00:02:48,577
immediately.

55
00:02:48,857 --> 00:02:51,090
It also gave them a
reason to pass it around.

56
00:02:51,370 --> 00:02:55,500
If somebody else reached an ending you
couldn't reach, their discovery might

57
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,365
explain what you had missed.

58
00:02:57,645 --> 00:03:02,375
A small game acquired a much larger
conversation around it, and that

59
00:03:02,395 --> 00:03:05,902
conversation was about to
become the advertisement.

60
00:03:06,001 --> 00:03:08,696
At the end, the view opens onto a street.

61
00:03:08,976 --> 00:03:10,809
A figure walks away from the camera.

62
00:03:11,064 --> 00:03:15,531
Names start appearing, and the face
finally turns into view: Norman

63
00:03:15,551 --> 00:03:16,334
Reedus.

64
00:03:16,614 --> 00:03:18,259
Then comes Silent Hills.

65
00:03:30,551 --> 00:03:35,509
The anonymous experiment is connected
to Hideo Kojima, Guillermo del Toro,

66
00:03:35,851 --> 00:03:37,784
and a series people already know.

67
00:03:38,064 --> 00:03:41,621
The reveal works because it
arrives after the experience.

68
00:03:41,914 --> 00:03:44,819
You aren't being asked to imagine whether
these people could make something

69
00:03:44,839 --> 00:03:45,559
frightening.

70
00:03:45,839 --> 00:03:47,384
You've just played the sales pitch.

71
00:03:47,651 --> 00:03:52,106
In an official KONAMI message, del Toro
talks about working with Kojima on the

72
00:03:52,126 --> 00:03:54,734
teaser and the ideas they were exchanging.

73
00:03:55,026 --> 00:03:59,524
That made the collaboration feel
tangible, even though the larger game

74
00:03:59,544 --> 00:04:00,676
remained a promise.

75
00:04:00,969 --> 00:04:05,396
And the ending itself draws a boundary
we need to keep: the playable teaser

76
00:04:05,614 --> 00:04:08,509
says it has no direct
relation to the main title.

77
00:04:08,801 --> 00:04:13,646
This corridor wasn't proof that a
finished Silent Hills existed somewhere.

78
00:04:13,926 --> 00:04:14,556
P.T.

79
00:04:14,576 --> 00:04:16,546
was the real thing people had played.

80
00:04:16,814 --> 00:04:20,384
Silent Hills was the future
they were imagining from it.

81
00:04:20,469 --> 00:04:25,437
On April twenty seventh, twenty fifteen,
Konami confirmed that Silent Hills

82
00:04:25,457 --> 00:04:26,714
would not continue.

83
00:04:27,007 --> 00:04:30,464
Here is the contemporary
report carrying the statement.

84
00:04:30,744 --> 00:04:34,089
One word is particularly
revealing: embryonic.

85
00:04:34,369 --> 00:04:38,837
It describes an early project, not a
completed masterpiece being pulled off a

86
00:04:38,857 --> 00:04:39,589
shelf.

87
00:04:39,882 --> 00:04:43,474
The disappointment was real, but the
game people were mourning was still

88
00:04:43,494 --> 00:04:45,214
partly the game in their heads.

89
00:04:45,494 --> 00:04:50,424
Kojima, del Toro, Reedus, and a very
effective teaser had given them

90
00:04:50,444 --> 00:04:51,489
plenty to imagine.

91
00:04:51,782 --> 00:04:55,549
We don't need to invent a secret meeting
or a hidden motive to explain why

92
00:04:55,569 --> 00:04:56,639
that announcement hurt.

93
00:04:56,907 --> 00:04:59,239
It closed off that
particular collaboration.

94
00:04:59,519 --> 00:05:03,002
It did not announce the end of
the entire Silent Hill series.

95
00:05:03,294 --> 00:05:07,737
Normally, a cancelled project can
leave behind a trailer or a demo as a

96
00:05:07,757 --> 00:05:08,514
souvenir.

97
00:05:08,794 --> 00:05:09,577
P.T.

98
00:05:09,632 --> 00:05:13,587
could have become something you showed
a friend with a short explanation of

99
00:05:13,607 --> 00:05:14,452
what might have been.

100
00:05:14,732 --> 00:05:18,974
But the distribution notice had already
put a deadline on the easy version of

101
00:05:18,994 --> 00:05:19,764
that story.

102
00:05:19,868 --> 00:05:23,376
The notice said distribution
would end on April twenty ninth.

103
00:05:23,656 --> 00:05:27,388
Keep that date separate from the
cancellation, and from what happened next.

104
00:05:27,668 --> 00:05:32,161
Removing a store listing and preventing
an existing customer from downloading

105
00:05:32,181 --> 00:05:33,988
again are different changes.

106
00:05:34,268 --> 00:05:37,563
At first, previous downloaders
could still reach P.T.

107
00:05:37,656 --> 00:05:38,563
through their history.

108
00:05:38,843 --> 00:05:43,001
Reporting on May sixth described that
route failing in North America too.

109
00:05:43,306 --> 00:05:45,613
This is why the actual
error screen matters.

110
00:05:45,906 --> 00:05:49,276
The issue wasn't simply that somebody
had searched for a game too late.

111
00:05:49,568 --> 00:05:51,951
The expected recovery path had failed.

112
00:05:52,218 --> 00:05:56,823
A copy already installed on a PlayStation
Four could still work; an empty

113
00:05:56,843 --> 00:06:00,701
drive could no longer be treated
as a temporary inconvenience.

114
00:06:00,993 --> 00:06:05,201
Konami did not remotely erase
every surviving installation.

115
00:06:05,493 --> 00:06:09,673
The software continued to exist, but
the ordinary way of obtaining it had

116
00:06:09,693 --> 00:06:12,338
become unreliable or unavailable.

117
00:06:12,631 --> 00:06:16,948
A game distributed as a free digital
download suddenly depended on a

118
00:06:16,968 --> 00:06:20,451
particular physical machine
keeping particular files.

119
00:06:20,743 --> 00:06:24,523
That's a very different relationship
with a console from assuming everything

120
00:06:24,543 --> 00:06:26,751
in your library can always be replaced.

121
00:06:26,863 --> 00:06:30,071
The consequences show up
in very ordinary places.

122
00:06:30,338 --> 00:06:35,071
On this GameFAQs thread, a player
describes upgrading to a larger hard drive

123
00:06:35,338 --> 00:06:37,008
and losing their installed demo.

124
00:06:37,276 --> 00:06:41,306
It's a personal account, not proof that
every hardware change had the same

125
00:06:41,326 --> 00:06:41,933
outcome.

126
00:06:42,201 --> 00:06:45,656
But look at the subject of the discussion:
their console keeps attempting a

127
00:06:45,676 --> 00:06:47,196
download that fails.

128
00:06:47,476 --> 00:06:52,031
This is the afterlife of the teaser in
a help forum, long after the launch

129
00:06:52,051 --> 00:06:52,646
mystery.

130
00:06:52,938 --> 00:06:56,881
A storage upgrade has become a conversation
about something that won't come

131
00:06:56,901 --> 00:06:57,496
back.

132
00:06:57,801 --> 00:07:00,946
That reverses the original
experience rather neatly.

133
00:07:01,213 --> 00:07:03,471
Inside the corridor, you wanted a way out.

134
00:07:03,763 --> 00:07:05,558
Outside it, you might want a way in.

135
00:07:05,838 --> 00:07:10,277
A recording can preserve the rooms, the
sound, and the ending, but somebody

136
00:07:10,297 --> 00:07:12,392
else is deciding where the camera looks.

137
00:07:12,697 --> 00:07:17,442
The software preserves the possibility
of making that decision yourself.

138
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:21,642
It can also preserve things nobody
thought to point the camera at.

139
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:26,318
Years after the cancellation, Lance
McDonald demonstrated why that difference

140
00:07:26,338 --> 00:07:26,983
matters.

141
00:07:27,057 --> 00:07:31,649
In this September twenty nineteen
investigation, McDonald changes how P.T.

142
00:07:31,669 --> 00:07:34,452
handles objects that appear and disappear.

143
00:07:34,744 --> 00:07:39,585
Watch the ordinary encounter first:
Lisa is visible down the hallway, then

144
00:07:39,605 --> 00:07:40,325
she's gone.

145
00:07:40,617 --> 00:07:42,175
Now look at the altered version.

146
00:07:42,467 --> 00:07:47,199
Her visibility stays on, and the
camera can approach a model the normal

147
00:07:47,219 --> 00:07:49,289
sequence only lets you glimpse.

148
00:07:49,569 --> 00:07:52,277
This isn't another person
recreating the house.

149
00:07:52,569 --> 00:07:56,137
It's an investigation of the original
software and the instructions

150
00:07:56,157 --> 00:07:58,239
controlling what the player can see.

151
00:07:58,532 --> 00:08:01,127
McDonald also examines the opening room.

152
00:08:01,407 --> 00:08:05,187
The normal opening directs your
attention through a narrow view of the

153
00:08:05,207 --> 00:08:05,864
doorway.

154
00:08:06,157 --> 00:08:10,162
By changing the way that moment runs,
he can inspect the character model

155
00:08:10,182 --> 00:08:11,002
outside it.

156
00:08:11,048 --> 00:08:14,505
The point isn't that a hidden model
automatically changes the plot.

157
00:08:14,785 --> 00:08:19,315
It's that a recording of one playthrough
cannot contain every possible

158
00:08:19,335 --> 00:08:21,855
investigation of the
program that produced it.

159
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:24,130
The cancellation had already happened.

160
00:08:24,410 --> 00:08:26,668
The advertising campaign was over.

161
00:08:26,948 --> 00:08:31,278
Yet the surviving teaser still had
details that somebody could reveal by

162
00:08:31,298 --> 00:08:34,068
asking a new question of
the same old software.

163
00:08:34,142 --> 00:08:38,697
Then came another test of the assumption
that a digital library moves forward

164
00:08:38,717 --> 00:08:39,300
with you.

165
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:42,772
In twenty twenty, reviewers
reported playing P.T.

166
00:08:42,792 --> 00:08:45,925
on early PlayStation Five review hardware.

167
00:08:46,242 --> 00:08:49,525
For a short time, the old teaser
appeared to have made the jump.

168
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:53,422
In the days that followed, the listing
changed to say it was playable on

169
00:08:53,442 --> 00:08:57,100
PlayStation Four, and the newer
console would no longer run it.

170
00:08:57,392 --> 00:09:00,725
Sony told Polygon that the
change was a publisher decision.

171
00:09:01,017 --> 00:09:04,860
These are historical reports about
review hardware in twenty twenty, not a

172
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:07,700
guide to what any workaround does today.

173
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:12,335
They show the problem clearly enough:
a newer machine did not guarantee a

174
00:09:12,355 --> 00:09:13,650
permanent route back.

175
00:09:13,942 --> 00:09:18,035
The age of your hardware and the
survival of an experience had become

176
00:09:18,055 --> 00:09:21,650
connected in a way a download
button never really explains.

177
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:26,077
There are two stories here, and the more
glamorous one can swallow the other.

178
00:09:26,370 --> 00:09:31,100
Silent Hills is the game people imagine:
the names, the possibilities, the

179
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:34,952
perfect horror project that never
had to survive being released.

180
00:09:35,245 --> 00:09:35,965
P.T.

181
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:37,802
is the thing we can actually examine.

182
00:09:38,095 --> 00:09:42,350
We can watch how it guides a camera,
compare the changing corridor, argue

183
00:09:42,370 --> 00:09:46,537
about the puzzles, and look inside the
software for decisions an ordinary

184
00:09:46,557 --> 00:09:47,802
player never sees.

185
00:09:48,082 --> 00:09:50,302
It doesn't need to be
flawless to be worth keeping.

186
00:09:50,582 --> 00:09:54,612
That is what makes its disappearance
from the normal download path more

187
00:09:54,632 --> 00:09:57,277
interesting than a cancelled
marketing campaign.

188
00:09:57,557 --> 00:10:01,740
The advertisement became an
experience with a life of its own.

189
00:10:02,070 --> 00:10:04,990
First, people shared it
to discover what it was.

190
00:10:05,270 --> 00:10:08,602
Later, they worried about whether
they would still be able to reach it.

191
00:10:08,895 --> 00:10:11,327
The hallway didn't vanish
from every console.

192
00:10:11,607 --> 00:10:15,477
What vanished was the reassuring assumption
that you could always come back.

193
00:10:15,745 --> 00:10:19,550
And for a game built around walking
through the same door again, that is a

194
00:10:19,570 --> 00:10:22,252
remarkably fitting thing to lose.
